{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-340465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065344/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebfd1e54b1499695e8e85754c00dc1125bae74bb342f367e54ebfcb6e246b817"} {"question" "q-340465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x-x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x+x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)-\\frac{1}{22}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "fil